    "answer": "(a) Quicklime & Reaction with Water:\n- **Quicklime:** Calcium oxide ($CaO$).\n- **Reaction:** When water is added to quicklime, a vigorous, hissing reaction occurs with the release of immense heat (strongly exothermic), boiling the water and forming a suspension of slaked lime (calcium hydroxide):\n$$CaO(s) + H_2O(l) \\rightarrow Ca(OH)_2(aq) + \\text{Heat}$$\n\n(b) Shiny White Wall Finish (Whitewashing Effect):\nWhen slaked lime [$Ca(OH)_2$] solution is painted onto walls, it reacts slowly over 2 to 3 days with carbon dioxide ($CO_2$) present in atmospheric air. This forms a thin, hard, shiny crystalline layer of **calcium carbonate ($CaCO_3$)**, giving the walls their brilliant white lustre:\n$$Ca(OH)_2(aq) + CO_2(g) \\rightarrow CaCO_3(s) [\\text{Shiny White Layer}] + H_2O(l)$$",
