    "question": "Identify the (i) Substance Oxidised, (ii) Substance Reduced, (iii) Oxidising Agent, and (iv) Reducing Agent in each of the following reactions:\n(a) Pb3O4 + 8HCl -> 3PbCl2 + Cl2 + 4H2O\n(b) 2H2S + SO2 -> 3S + 2H2O\n(c) Fe2O3 + 3CO -> 2Fe + 3CO2\n(d) 2Na + H2 -> 2NaH\n(e) CuO + H2 -> Cu + H2O",
    "answer": "### Comprehensive Redox Analysis:\n\n(a) $\\text{Pb}_3\\text{O}_4 + 8\\text{HCl} \\rightarrow 3\\text{PbCl}_2 + \\text{Cl}_2 + 4\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$:\n- **Substance Oxidised:** $HCl$ (loss of hydrogen / chloride oxidized to $Cl_2$)\n- **Substance Reduced:** $Pb_3O_4$ (loses oxygen)\n- **Oxidising Agent:** $Pb_3O_4$\n- **Reducing Agent:** $HCl$\n\n(b) $2\\text{H}_2\\text{S} + \\text{SO}_2 \\rightarrow 3\\text{S} + 2\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$:\n- **Substance Oxidised:** $H_2S$ (loses hydrogen to form $S$)\n- **Substance Reduced:** $SO_2$ (loses oxygen to form $S$)\n- **Oxidising Agent:** $SO_2$\n- **Reducing Agent:** $H_2S$\n\n(c) $\\text{Fe}_2\\text{O}_3 + 3\\text{CO} \\rightarrow 2\\text{Fe} + 3\\text{CO}_2$:\n- **Substance Oxidised:** $CO$ (gains oxygen to become $CO_2$)\n- **Substance Reduced:** $Fe_2O_3$ (loses oxygen to form $Fe$)\n- **Oxidising Agent:** $Fe_2O_3$\n- **Reducing Agent:** $CO$\n\n(d) $2\\text{Na} + \\text{H}_2 \\rightarrow 2\\text{NaH}$:\n- **Substance Oxidised:** $Na$ (loses electrons to form $Na^+$)\n- **Substance Reduced:** $H_2$ (gains electrons to form hydride ion $H^-$)\n- **Oxidising Agent:** $H_2$\n- **Reducing Agent:** $Na$\n\n(e) $\\text{CuO} + \\text{H}_2 \\rightarrow \\text{Cu} + \\text{H}_2\\text{O}$:\n- **Substance Oxidised:** $H_2$ (gains oxygen to form $H_2O$)\n- **Substance Reduced:** $CuO$ (loses oxygen to form $Cu$)\n- **Oxidising Agent:** $CuO$\n- **Reducing Agent:** $H_2$",
    "explanation": "Reason: Substance oxidized = Reducing agent; Substance reduced = Oxidizing agent. All 4 terms are reactants.",
    "formula": "LEO: Loss of Electrons = Oxidation | GER: Gain of Electrons = Reduction",
    "examinerNote": "In (d), hydrogen acts as an oxidizing agent because sodium is more electropositive.",
